The real original error, even with the numbering logic fine, is that the returned closure is borrowing `prefix` and `next` from `make_labeler`, but it is returned from the function. That would give the compiler an error about returning a closure that references local data, because those locals would be dropped when `make_labeler` ends.

`move` fixes that by moving `prefix` and `next` into the closure, so the closure owns its captured state instead of borrowing it. The closure can then live on after `make_labeler` returns.

`disk` and `net` still each get their own separate counter, because each call to `make_labeler` creates a distinct closure with its own moved-in `next`.

Fixed program:

```rust
fn make_labeler(kind: &str, start: u32) -> impl FnMut() -> String {
    let prefix = kind.to_uppercase();
    let mut next = start;
    move || {
        next += 1;
        format!("{prefix}-{next}")
    }
}

fn main() {
    let mut disk = make_labeler("disk", 7);
    let mut net = make_labeler("net", 0);
    println!("{} {} {}", disk(), disk(), net());
}
```
