Here is a version I’d put in:

```tsx
import { useId, useMemo, useState } from "react";
import type { KeyboardEvent } from "react";
import type { Label } from "./types";

interface LabelPickerProps {
  labels: Label[];
  selected: string[];
  onToggle: (id: string) => void;
}

export function LabelPicker({ labels, selected, onToggle }: LabelPickerProps) {
  const inputId = useId();
  const listId = useId();
  const [text, setText] = useState("");
  const [active, setActive] = useState(0);

  const matches = useMemo(
    () => labels.filter((l) => l.name.toLowerCase().includes(text.toLowerCase())),
    [labels, text],
  );

  const hasResults = matches.length > 0;
  const activeLabelId = hasResults ? matches[Math.min(active, matches.length - 1)]?.id : undefined;

  const onKeyDown = (e: KeyboardEvent<HTMLInputElement>) => {
    if (!hasResults) return;

    if (e.key === "ArrowDown") {
      e.preventDefault();
      setActive((i) => Math.min(i + 1, matches.length - 1));
    }

    if (e.key === "ArrowUp") {
      e.preventDefault();
      setActive((i) => Math.max(i - 1, 0));
    }

    if (e.key === "Enter") {
      e.preventDefault();
      const item = matches[Math.min(active, matches.length - 1)];
      if (item) onToggle(item.id);
    }

    if (e.key === "Escape") {
      e.preventDefault();
      setText("");
      setActive(0);
    }
  };

  return (
    <div className="picker">
      <label htmlFor={inputId} className="picker-label">
        Search labels
      </label>
      <input
        id={inputId}
        className="picker-input"
        type="text"
        value={text}
        onChange={(e) => {
          setText(e.target.value);
          setActive(0);
        }}
        onKeyDown={onKeyDown}
        role="combobox"
        aria-autocomplete="list"
        aria-expanded={text.length > 0 && hasResults}
        aria-controls={listId}
        aria-activedescendant={activeLabelId}
        placeholder="Search labels"
      />

      {text && (
        <ul className="picker-list" id={listId} role="listbox" aria-label="Matching labels">
          {matches.length === 0 ? (
            <li className="option" aria-disabled="true">
              No matching labels
            </li>
          ) : (
            matches.map((l, i) => (
              <li
                key={l.id}
                id={l.id}
                role="option"
                aria-selected={selected.includes(l.id)}
                className={i === active ? "option active" : "option"}
                onMouseDown={(e) => {
                  e.preventDefault();
                  onToggle(l.id);
                }}
                onMouseEnter={() => setActive(i)}
              >
                <span className="swatch" aria-hidden="true" style={{ background: l.color }} />
                <span>{l.name}</span>
                {selected.includes(l.id) && <span className="check" aria-hidden="true">✓</span>}
              </li>
            ))
          )}
        </ul>
      )}
    </div>
  );
}
```

What changed and why:

1. **Real label for the input**
   The old version only had a placeholder. That is weak for accessibility because placeholder text disappears and is not a reliable accessible name. I added a `<label>` so screen readers announce it properly.

2. **Combobox semantics on the input**
   The input now exposes itself as a combobox with `aria-controls`, `aria-expanded`, and `aria-activedescendant`. That gives NVDA a proper model for a text field with a popup list of options.

3. **Listbox/options instead of clickable divs**
   The suggestions are now `ul[role=listbox]` with `li[role=option]`. That is much better than plain divs. The active row can be announced, and selected state can be conveyed with `aria-selected`.

4. **Keyboard handling on the input**
   ArrowUp/ArrowDown move the active option. Enter toggles the active option instead of submitting the outer form. Escape clears the query, which is a pretty common pattern.

5. **No focus leaving the input to browse options**
   This is important: focus stays on the search box, and the active option is tracked with `aria-activedescendant`. That is the simplest model for keyboard and SR users, and it avoids tabbing through every suggestion.

6. **Mouse interaction still works**
   I kept click-to-toggle via `onMouseDown` so clicking a suggestion doesn’t blur the input first and accidentally submit or close the dialog.

