A trait object needs a single fixed vtable layout, but a generic method has no single monomorphic function body to put in the vtable. The caller must know `V` at compile time, while `dyn Store` is meant to erase the concrete type.

Two fixes:
1. Make `put` take a trait object / erased type, for example `fn put(&mut self, key: &str, value: &dyn ToString)` or `&str`.
   - Cost: less flexibility and sometimes extra conversions/copying; using trait objects can also mean dynamic dispatch.

2. Make the trait generic instead of using a trait object, e.g. `fn fill<S: Store>(store: &mut S)` and keep `put` generic, or use an enum for the value type.
   - Cost: with a generic `fill`, you lose the ability to mix different `Store` implementors behind one `dyn Store`; with an enum, you must enumerate all supported value kinds up front.
