[entity 801 · Lesson · lesson]
## 1. Where patterns live

You have used patterns since 01 without calling them that. A pattern describes the shape of a value: it can test parts of the value and bind other parts to names. The same pattern syntax works everywhere a pattern is allowed:

PlaceExampleMust always match?

`match` arms`Some(n) => ..`no (but all arms together must cover every value)

`if let` / `while let``while let Some(top) = stack.pop()`no

`let ... else``let Ok(n) = text.parse::<i32>() else { return; };`no (else must leave)

`let``let (a, b) = (1, "one");`yes

`for``for (name, qty) in [("pen", 2), ("ink", 0)]`yes

function parameters`fn show(&(x, y): &(i32, i32))`yes

Closure parameters are patterns too; closures arrive in 10. `matches!(value, pattern)` turns a pattern into a `bool`: `matches!(c, 'a'..='z')`.

This lesson adapts The Rust Programming Language, chapter 19 and the Rust by Example pages on `match` (MIT/Apache-2.0). Open `places.rs` and `syntax.rs` alongside it.

## 2. Refutable and irrefutable

A pattern that matches every possible value of its type is irrefutable. One that might fail is refutable. `let`, `for` and parameters need irrefutable patterns, because there is no "else" to go to.

```
let (x, y) = (1, 2);                 // irrefutable: every 2-tuple fits
let [first, second, third] = [7, 8, 9]; // irrefutable: an array's length is part of its type
let maybe: Option<i32> = Some(5);
// let Some(v) = maybe;              // error[E0005]: refutable pattern in local binding
```

A slice is different from an array. The type `&[i32]` says nothing about the length, which is only known at run time. So a slice pattern is refutable, even `[head, ..]`, because the slice may be empty:

```
let items: &[i32] = &[1, 2, 3];
// let [head, ..] = items;           // error[E0005]: pattern `&[]` not covered
let [head, ..] = items else {
    return;
};
```

The reverse mistake, an irrefutable pattern in `if let`, compiles with a warning: the `if` is pointless.

## 3. Literals, `|` and ranges

```
let kind = match n {
    1 | 2 => "one or two",      // either value
    3..=9 => "three to nine",   // inclusive range
    10..100 => "two digits",    // half-open: 10 up to 99
    100.. => "big",             // open: 100 and up
    _ => "zero or negative",
};
```

Ranges work on integers and `char` (`'a'..='z'`). `..=0` means "up to and including 0". Watch the difference between `1..99` (stops at 98) and `1..=99`.

## 4. Names versus consts

This is the most common pattern trap. A lowercase name in a pattern always creates a new binding. It matches anything and never compares against a variable that happens to have the same name:

```
let wanted = 5;
match 9 {
    wanted => println!("bound wanted = {wanted}"), // matches 9, prints 9
}
```

The arm declares a brand new `wanted` that shadows the outer one, exactly like `let wanted = 9;` would. Any arms after it are unreachable (the compiler warns).

A `const` (by convention UPPER_CASE) is different: it is a fixed value, so in a pattern it is compared:

```
const ORIGIN_X: i32 = 0;
match p {
    Point { x: ORIGIN_X, y } => println!("on the y axis at {y}"), // x must equal 0
    Point { x, y } => println!("at ({x}, {y})"),
}
```

To compare against a variable, use a guard: `n if n == wanted => ..`. Patterns test shapes and constant values; guards test anything at run time.

## 5. Destructuring and strings

Patterns take apart tuples, structs, enums and any nesting of them. `_` ignores one part; `..` ignores the rest.

```
match msg {
    Message::Move { x, y: 0 } => println!("horizontal to {x}"),
    Message::Move { x, .. } => println!("move, x = {x}"),
    Message::Color(r, _, _) => println!("red part {r}"),
    Message::Pair((a, b), _) => println!("nested {a} {b}"),
    _ => {}
}
```

String literal patterns like `"go"` have type `&str`. A `String` is a different type, so match on a `&str` view of it with `as_str()`:

```
let cmd = String::from("go");
match cmd.as_str() {
    "go" | "run" => println!("moving"),
    other => println!("unknown {other}"),
}
```

## 6. Guards and `@` bindings

A guard is `if condition` after a pattern. The arm runs only if the pattern fits and the guard is true. If the guard is false, matching simply continues with the next arm.

A guard after an or-pattern applies to the whole `a | b`, not just the last alternative:

```
(x, 0) | (0, x) if x < 0 => format!("negative axis {x}"),
// means: ((x, 0) | (0, x)) if x < 0
```

Both alternatives must bind the same names with the same types, because the arm body uses them either way.

`name @ subpattern` tests a value against a sub-pattern and binds the whole value to `name` at once:

```
match v {
    whole @ Some(1..=9) => println!("small {whole:?}"), // whole: Option<i32>
    Some(n @ 10..) => println!("large {n}"),          // n: i32, known to be >= 10
    whole => println!("other {whole:?}"),
}
match word {
    c @ ("go" | "run") => println!("move ({c})"),
    _ => {}
}
```

## 7. Exhaustiveness, again

The checker reasons about patterns only. A guarded arm covers nothing, because the compiler cannot know what the guard will say. Walk a value through a match like this:

- Try arm 1: does the pattern fit? If not, go to the next arm.

- If it fits, is there a guard, and is it true? If false, go to the next arm.

- Both yes: bind the names, run the body, stop.

Integer ranges are checked exactly, so a gap like a missing `100` between `1..=99` and `101..=255` is reported by value:

```
error[E0004]: non-exhaustive patterns: `&Signal::Level(100_u8)` not covered
```

Fixing a hole with `_` makes the error go away but hides the bug (the off-by-one is still there) and stops the compiler from flagging variants added later. Close the hole explicitly when you can.

## 8. Slice patterns

Slice patterns match on the length and the elements at once:

```
fn ends(items: &[&str]) -> String {
    match items {
        [] => String::from("none"),                       // length 0
        [only] => format!("only {only}"),                 // length 1
        [first, .., last] => format!("{first}..{last}"),  // length 2 or more
    }
}

fn sum(items: &[i32]) -> i32 {
    match items {
        [] => 0,
        [head, tail @ ..] => head + sum(tail), // head: &i32, tail: &[i32]
    }
}
```

- `..` stands for "zero or more elements" and may appear once per slice pattern.

- Matching a `&[T]`, single elements bind as `&T` and `rest @ ..` binds as `&[T]`: a view into the same memory, nothing is copied.

- To decide if a match is complete, ask what the smallest length each arm accepts is. `[]`, `[x]` and `[a, ..]` or `[a, b, ..]`-style arms that cover 0, 1 and "2 or more" are complete.

- A `Vec` is not a slice. Match `v.as_slice()` or `&v[..]`; `match v { [a, b] => .. }` fails with E0529.

## 9. Binding modes: what a name binds to

When you match through a reference, the same pattern binds differently. Take

```
enum Slot { Full { label: String, count: u32 }, Empty }
let first: &Slot = &slots[0];
```

You write`label` is`count` isWhy

`if let Slot::Full { label, count } = first``&String``&u32`non-reference pattern on a `&Slot`: fields bind by reference (default binding mode)

`if let &Slot::Full { count, .. } = first`-`u32`the `&` pattern matches the reference itself; Copy fields bind by value

`if let Slot::Full { ref label, .. } = *first``&String`-matching the place `*first` by value; `ref` borrows instead of moving

`if let Slot::Full { label, .. } = *first`error[E0507]: cannot move out of a shared referenceby-value binding of a `String` would move it out of borrowed data

Through `&mut`, the default mode gives `&mut` bindings, so you can change fields in place:

```
for slot in &mut slots {
    if let Slot::Full { count, .. } = slot {
        *count += 10; // count: &mut u32
    }
}
```

An explicit `&mut` pattern resets the mode: `if let &mut Slot::Full { mut count, .. } = slot` binds `count` as a copy (`u32`). Changing it changes the copy, not the slot.

Edition 2024 rule: once a pattern is already borrowing implicitly (you matched a reference with a non-reference pattern), writing `ref` or `ref mut` inside it is an error: `cannot explicitly borrow within an implicitly-borrowing pattern`. Use `ref` only when matching a place by value, like `*first` or `slots[0]`.

## 10. Check yourself

- Why can `let (a, b) = pair;` and `let [x, y] = arr;` work, but `let [x, ..] = slice;` not?

- Why does `wanted => ...` match everything, but `MAX => ...` does not?

- Matching `&Option<String>` with `Some(s)`: what type is `s`, and why?

- A value fits an arm's pattern but its guard is false. What happens next?

Exercises: `ex1_scores.rs` (tuple patterns, ranges, guards and `@`), `ex2_slices.rs` (slice patterns and recursion), `ex3_slots.rs` (binding modes through `&` and `&mut`).

