Polymorphism here is through dynamic dispatch. Each element in the vector is a `Box<dyn Stage>`, so when `run` or `describe` calls `stage.apply(...)` or `stage.name()`, Rust uses the vtable attached to that trait object to jump to the concrete implementation for that particular boxed value. That is why the same loop can handle `Add`, `Mul`, and `Neg` correctly even though they are different concrete types.

A `Vec<T>` with `T: Stage` would not work for this mixed collection because a `Vec` stores one concrete element type throughout. `Add`, `Mul`, and `Neg` are different types with different sizes, so they cannot all be stored directly as the same `T`. Boxing them erases the concrete type behind a uniform pointer-sized handle, which makes the vector homogeneous while still letting each element dispatch to the right method at runtime.
