A `Box<dyn Fn(u32) -> bool>` needs the closure inside it to be callable later through a trait object, which means it must not borrow some short-lived stack thing. The closure has to own anything it captures, or at least be valid for the whole box. Here the problem is that `limit` is only borrowed by the closure, so the boxed value would outlive that borrow.

`move` did not already fix it because it only moves the reference itself, not the thing behind it. So the closure still carries `&u32`, and that reference is still tied to the local `limit` in `main`.

Fixing it means copying the number into the closure so the boxed thing owns its own limit value.

```rust
struct Filters {
    rules: Vec<Box<dyn Fn(u32) -> bool>>,
}

impl Filters {
    fn add_limit(&mut self, limit: &u32) {
        let limit = *limit;
        self.rules.push(Box::new(move |n| n <= limit));
    }

    fn passes(&self, n: u32) -> bool {
        self.rules.iter().all(|rule| rule(n))
    }
}

fn main() {
    let mut filters = Filters { rules: Vec::new() };
    filters.rules.push(Box::new(|n| n % 2 == 0));
    let limit = 10;
    filters.add_limit(&limit);
    let items = [4, 7, 12, 8];
    let kept: Vec<u32> = items.into_iter().filter(|&n| filters.passes(n)).collect();
    println!("{kept:?}");
}
```
