Why will it not compile? What is the smallest fix to `run_twice`? And if someone changed the bound to `impl FnOnce()` instead, where would the error move to, and why?

messages.content:
It won’t compile because the closure `bump` mutably captures `count` (`count += 1`), so it implements `FnMut`, not `Fn`. But `run_twice` requires an `Fn`, which promises it can be called through an immutable borrow and without mutating its captured environment.

The smallest fix to `run_twice` is to change the bound to `FnMut` and make the parameter mutable:

```rust
fn run_twice<F: FnMut()>(mut f: F) {
    f();
    f();
}
```

If someone changed the bound to `impl FnOnce()` instead, then the error would move to the second call inside `run_twice`. `FnOnce` only guarantees the closure can be called one time, because it may consume captured values when called. So `f();` is allowed once, but the second `f();` becomes the problem.
