    last_stage_performance: Opened with a generic 'keep the top half small, defer sleeping work' answer that named nothing from the repo or log. With a direct push they located the actual path: qadc_hardirq -> qadc_wdog_kick -> qadc_wdog_period taking mutex_lock(&st->cfg_lock), and quoted the mutex.c sleeping-function line and the trace ordering. Read preempt_count 10001 and irqs_disabled 128 as hard IRQ context, though loosely ('interrupted code was already in an interrupt-disabled section') and never identified swapper/0 or that the idle task was interrupted. Never touched the lockdep report at all: no mention of {HARDIRQ-ON-W} -> {IN-HARDIRQ-W}, of cfg_lock being registered in qadc_probe, or of odr_store and qadc_reset_work being the process-context holders. The account of why production seems fine stayed at 'timing lines up badly' and never reached the real mechanism: an actual wait on a held mutex is a sleep in interrupt context, and if the owner is the task interrupted on CPU0 the box hangs. Fix proposed (hard handler only timestamps and counts, kick the watchdog from the thread) is correct and was offered unprompted, but mod_timer itself was wrongly implied to be the sleeping part early on.
  [probe] asked: From a second lockdep report naming latest_show against qadc_hardirq: say what lockdep is telling you, what actually happens to a unit in the field, the fix, and then every function that touches st->lock with its execution context and the right lock call.
    last_stage_performance: Read the report's shape correctly at a high level but never quoted it: no mention of the IN-HARDIRQ-W versus HARDIRQ-ON-W usage pair, no HE1, no cat/731, no note that the registration site was qadc_hardirq via _raw_spin_lock, and no observation that this particular trace came from the other CPU so lockdep flagged the pattern without an actual hang. First pass on the field failure was generic: latency, dropped samples, missed edges, plus a wrong claim that the collision needs the other CPU. After a direct push they did reach the same-CPU self-deadlock and described the CPU being wedged while the handler cannot finish, though they never closed the loop that the interrupted reader can never resume to release it, so that CPU is gone for good, and they did not use the README line about the GPIO interrupt being serviced on CPU0 to explain why units hang only rarely. The walkthrough of st->lock users was mostly right on context labels: hardirq plain lock, thread irqsave, sysfs irqsave. Two errors stood: qadc_wdog_expired was left on plain spin_lock even after being challenged, with the candidate correctly saying context not size decides it but then stopping at 'cannot sleep' rather than asking what can interrupt a timer callback on that CPU; and stats_show was given a lock variant rather than noticed as taking no lock at all over a 64-bit counter on a 32-bit CPU. Also never questioned whether the hard handler should be touching st->lock at all. Correct instinct about snapshotting and formatting outside the lock, offered unprompted.
  [probe] asked: From /proc/interrupts over 10 s and a logic capture of RDY, I2C and a hard-handler debug pin: explain what the counts and the capture show, which lines in the driver cause it, and the fix.
    last_stage_performance: Reached the right headline diagnosis fast and unprompted: shared level-low line, interrupt re-asserting because the request is not oneshot, and the fix of making the threaded handler the only worker with the line kept masked until the thread has read the data. Pointed at the correct two places, the request_threaded_irq call in qadc_probe and the 8-byte read in qadc_irq_thread. But named not one number or line from the evidence: not the 243,963 interrupts in 10 s against 2,000 conversions, not the 82 and 162 hard-handler entries per conversion, not the 3.83 us re-entry interval, not the comment about the dummy primary handler being used to get around the kernel's refusal of a NULL primary without oneshot. Entirely missed the second half of the stage: the repeated identical byte patterns in the decode, reads happening after RDY is already high, samples rising 5,002 and 4,002 for 1,000 conversions each, and the fact that the thread pushes a sample and returns IRQ_HANDLED regardless of whether its own device asserted, so each driver samples on the other's conversions. Also did not note that the fix flag must be agreed by every sharer. Diagnosis was shaped correctly but stayed at the level of recall rather than reading the data.

