(2) `STOP` is a constant pattern, so it compares against the value of `cmd.as_str()`. But `fallback` there is a new pattern binding, not a comparison, because lower-case names in patterns usually mean “bind this value to a variable” unless they’re written in a way that forces constant matching.

(3) To make the `fallback` arm compare while keeping `match`, rename the binding and compare explicitly against the argument, for example:

```rust
fn route(cmd: &String, fallback: &str) -> String {
    match cmd.as_str() {
        STOP => String::from("halt"),
        c @ ("go" | "run") => format!("move ({c})"),
        f if f == fallback => format!("fallback {fallback}"),
        other => format!("unknown {other}"),
    }
}
```

That way the arm only says `fallback` when the command equals the `fallback` argument.
