A `Box<dyn Fn(u32) -> bool>` means the closure must be callable later, after `add_limit` returns, so it cant borrow a local that will go out of scope. In `p3.rs`, `add_limit` takes `limit: &u32` and the closure captures that reference, so the box would store a closure with a borrowed `limit`. That makes the closure not `'static`, and the trait object in the vector needs to live independently of the stack frame.

The `move` only moved the reference value `&u32` into the closure; it did not move the actual `u32` out of `main`. So the closure still points at stack data owned by `main`, and Rust rejects it if the boxed closure may outlive that borrow.

Fix: copy the `u32` into the closure, so it owns the limit value itself.
