`step.run(&mut out)` is rejected in `p4.rs` because `run` is declared as `fn run(self: Box<Self>, ...)` in the trait, so it needs ownership of the boxed step. But `self.steps.iter()` gives `&Box<dyn Step>`, just a shared borrow, not an owned box, so Rust cant move the step out through `iter()`.

Fix `finish` by taking the steps out of the vec and consuming them, eg with `drain(..)`:

- each step is moved out of `self.steps`
- then `run` can take the `Box<Self>` by value
- `main` stays the same
- after `finish`, `job.steps.len()` is `0`, because all boxed steps were drained and consumed

On design:
- if new kinds of steps are added often, I would keep `Box<dyn Step>`: it lets you add new concrete step types without changing the pipeline storage.
- if new operations are added often, I would switch to an `enum`: then you can match on variants and add new behaviour in one place, without needing trait-object plumbing.

That means: many new types => trait object; many new methods/ops => enum usually fits better.
