`step.run(&mut out)` is rejected because `run` takes `self: Box<Self>`, so calling it needs ownership of the boxed step. In `iter()` we only have `&Box<dyn Step>`, just a shared borrow, so Rust will not let us move the step out through that reference.

`finish` can be fixed by taking the steps out of `self` and consuming them one by one:

- use `std::mem::take(&mut self.steps)` to replace the vector with an empty one
- iterate over the owned `Box<dyn Step>` values
- call `run` on each owned box

After that, `job.steps.len()` is `0`, which is right because `finish` consumes the stored steps and leaves the job empty.

Design-wise: if new kinds of steps are added often, I would keep `Box<dyn Step>` because adding a new concrete step type is easy and does not require changing an existing enum. If new operations are added often, I would switch to an enum, because with an enum you usually need to update every variant-handling `match`, but it is still often better when the set of step kinds is fixed and you want static dispatch and exhaustiveness.
