Each `Box<dyn Stage>` stores a fat pointer: one part points to the concrete value, and the other points to the vtable for that concrete type. `Pipeline::run` and `describe` are compiled once against the trait object type, but at each call like `stage.apply(x)` or `stage.name()`, Rust looks up the function pointer in the vtable for that particular boxed value and dispatches to the right implementation.

A generic `Vec<T: Stage>` could not hold `add`, `mul`, and `neg` together because a generic vector has one concrete element type `T` chosen at compile time. All elements must have the same size and type, while the pipeline needs to mix different concrete stage types in one collection.
